Sure!  In this case, you’d use a "for" loop.  Here’s an example:

int i = 0;  // initialization
for (i = 1; i <= 10; i++) {
    printf("The value of i is: %d\n", i);
}  // output

In this example, the loop runs five times, because i is initialized with 1, increased by one in each iteration, and the loop condition is true 10 times in the end.  You can also use other types of loops:

for (i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
    j = i + 1;
    printf("The value of j is: %d\n", j);
}

This loop also runs five times, but i is decreased by one each time.  To include a counter, you can use the "int" data type, but don’t go over 2147483647 for your counter, since anything that big is negative:

int i = 0;
for (i = 1; i <= 10 && i <= 2147483647; i++) {
    printf("The value of i is: %d\n", i);
}

Finally, if you want the value of some expression to determine whether the loop should continue, you can specify it in parentheses:

for (;;) { // do something here }
printf("Invoked the loop");
for (i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
    if (i == 5) {
        break;  // stop looping here
    }
    printf("The value of i is: %d\n", i);
}

